{"type":"text","timestamp":1791981879040,"sessionID":"ses_ec5903378ffedLI7p71UGYWhQJ","part":{"id":"prt_13a70d50c001ogClksjFcuK1wk","sessionID":"ses_ec5903378ffedLI7p71UGYWhQJ","messageID":"msg_13a70d15c001kLF3vFC9w5DTfC","type":"text","text":"---\n\n# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** The report should display a warning to users when its content may be out of date (stale). The product owner has not yet decided two material facts: (1) how old a report must be before it is considered stale, and (2) whether the warning is mandatory (always shown when the threshold is met) or optional (can be dismissed or suppressed).\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The report (a named system component or UI surface) is responsible for displaying the staleness warning. The report is treated as the single responsible system unless additional information establishes that a separate service, scheduler, or notification subsystem owns this behavior.\n\n**Source type:** This is a partial goal statement with two explicitly unresolved product decisions. It is not a normative obligation yet. The uncertainty is acknowledged by the product owner and must not be resolved by assumption.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 — Staleness threshold (blocking)**\nWhat age threshold makes a report stale? For example: more than 24 hours since last data refresh, more than N business days since generation, or some other measurable criterion? The implementability contract and verification contract cannot be completed without this value and its units.\n\n**Q2 — Mandatory vs. optional warning (blocking)**\nMust the warning appear whenever the threshold is met (mandatory, with no user-dismissal path), or may the user dismiss or suppress it? This determines whether the requirement is an unconditional obligation or a conditional one, and whether a separate requirement is needed to govern dismissal.\n\n**Q3 — Warning content and form**\nWhat does the warning communicate? For example, must it state the data age, the threshold, a last-refresh timestamp, or only a generic \"may be stale\" label? This affects implementability and verification.\n\n**Q4 — Report identity**\nDoes \"the report\" refer to a single report type, every report in the system, or a configurable subset? If multiple report types exist, does the staleness rule apply uniformly?\n\n**Q5 — What clock or event defines age?**\nIs age measured from the time the underlying data was last refreshed, the time the report was generated, or another observable event? Two implementers may choose differently without this definition.\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1\n\n- **Status:** `needs clarification`\n- **Template:** unresolved — cannot select between Event-driven, State-driven, Unwanted behavior, or Ubiquitous without knowing whether the warning is mandatory, what triggers it, and what threshold defines \"stale.\"\n- **Requirement:** *(Drafting is blocked. A provisional sketch is shown below for review only — it is not a normative sentence.)*\n\n  > **Provisional sketch (not normative):** While the report data age exceeds [THRESHOLD — undefined], the report shall display a staleness warning.\n\n  The bracketed value is a material open decision. The EARS pattern, the normative sentence, and both contracts cannot be finalized until Q1 and Q2 are answered.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The product owner wants users to know when the report content may not reflect current data, reducing the risk of decisions based on stale information. The product owner has not committed to a threshold or to whether the warning is always shown.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: The report surface (UI, document, or API response — form not yet established).\n  - Responsible system: The report.\n  - Actor: The user viewing the report.\n  - Affected interfaces: The report display interface (visual, document, or API). Specific interface not yet defined.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: A measurable \"data age\" value — the elapsed time since an observable reference event (Q5). **Open: reference event undefined.**\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: The condition that makes a report stale. **Open: staleness threshold and units undefined (Q1).**\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Display of a staleness warning. **Open: mandatory vs. optional undefined (Q2); warning content undefined (Q3).**\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: **Open: threshold value and units undefined (Q1).**\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Stale,\" \"warning,\" \"data age,\" \"mandatory,\" \"report\" — all unresolved. See Q1–Q5.\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: **Open: cannot specify setup without the threshold (Q1) and the reference event (Q5).**\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: The presence or absence of a staleness warning on the report surface.\n  - Expected result: Warning is displayed when data age exceeds the threshold. **Open: pass/fail boundary undefined.**\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: **Open: threshold undefined (Q1).**\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Case where age equals threshold exactly (boundary); case where age is below threshold (warning absent); case where age data is unavailable. All unresolvable without Q1 and Q5.\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: A verifiable timestamp or elapsed-time value observable at the report boundary.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: yes — the warning is a visible property of the report surface, provided the age value is also observable there.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: N/A.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**SR-A — Boundary companion** (relates to Candidate 1)\n*Missing behavior:* What the report shall do when the data age cannot be determined (e.g., no timestamp is available). Without this, two implementers would make different choices: one suppresses the warning, one shows it unconditionally.\n*Suggestion:* If the report cannot determine whether its data is current, then the report shall display a staleness warning.\n*Reason:* Absence of an age value is arguably the most conservative case for staleness and should be explicitly addressed before implementation.\n\n**SR-B — Required companion** (relates to Candidate 1, conditional on Q2)\n*Missing behavior:* If the warning is determined to be dismissible (Q2 = optional), a requirement is needed to define how and for how long a user may suppress the warning, and whether it reappears on the next session or the next threshold crossing.\n*Suggestion:* Add a companion requirement governing dismissal scope, persistence, and re-display conditions once the mandatory/optional decision is made.\n*Reason:* Without this companion, the dismissal behavior is undefined and two implementers will produce different results.\n\n**SR-C — Operational companion** (relates to Candidate 1)\n*Missing behavior:* The report data-age value must be observable at the report boundary for the staleness condition to be verifiable. If age is not currently surfaced, a requirement to expose it may be needed.\n*Suggestion:* Consider whether a separate requirement is needed for the report to expose the data-refresh timestamp (or computed age) so that the staleness condition can be independently verified.\n*Reason:* Without an observable age value, an evaluator examining only the report boundary cannot verify the warning logic.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n**Finding 1 — Gap: threshold is undefined**\nCandidate 1 cannot be reconciled with any other system behavior because the staleness threshold is not defined. If a threshold is later introduced in a different requirement (e.g., a data-refresh SLA), it should be confirmed whether the staleness definition here must align with that value.\n\n**Finding 2 — Precedence question: mandatory vs. optional**\nIf the warning is optional (Q2), an explicit precedence is needed between the \"show warning\" obligation and any user preference or role-based permission to suppress it. Without this, two valid interpretations conflict: one always shows the warning to all users; one respects user-level suppression settings.\n\nNo conflicts or duplicates exist in the current candidate set because only one candidate has been drafted.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1 (provisional):** \"The report\" refers to a single, identifiable system component or UI surface. If multiple report types exist, this assumption must be revisited.\n\n**A2 (provisional):** \"Age\" is a duration measured from a single observable reference event (e.g., last data refresh). If age is derived from multiple events, the calculation rule must be defined.\n\n**A3 (provisional):** The staleness condition is evaluated at report-display time, not at a scheduled interval. This assumption affects whether the requirement is event-driven (triggered by display) or state-driven (maintained continuously). It must be confirmed by the product owner.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe product owner's intent is to protect users from acting on outdated report data. The warning is the proposed mechanism. The product owner has explicitly deferred two decisions: the staleness threshold and whether the warning is mandatory. These are not implementation details; they are normative policy choices that determine the EARS pattern, the normative sentence, and the verification criteria. They must be resolved before a normative requirement can be finalized.\n\nThe source statement \"the report *probably* should show a warning\" uses modal language (\"probably,\" \"should\") that signals deliberation, not an obligation. This has been treated as a goal in need of clarification, not a normative `shall` obligation.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Item | Status | Blocking reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Candidate 1 | `needs clarification` | Q1 (threshold), Q2 (mandatory/optional), Q3 (content), Q5 (reference event) all open; both contracts incomplete |\n| SR-A | suggestion | Pending acceptance and Q1/Q5 resolution |\n| SR-B | suggestion | Pending Q2 decision |\n| SR-C | suggestion | Pending Q4/interface definition |\n\n**No candidate is ready for review.** All candidates and supporting requirements remain blocked until the product owner answers at minimum **Q1** (staleness threshold) and **Q2** (mandatory vs. optional). Answering **Q3** (warning content) and **Q5** (reference event) is also required to pass the implementability and verification contracts.\n\n---\n\nThe package has been written to `artifacts/result.md`. Key decisions made during elicitation:\n\n- The source language (\"probably should\") was treated as a **goal under deliberation**, not a normative obligation — no `shall` sentence was invented.\n- Candidate 1 is held at `needs clarification` because **both the implementability contract and verification contract have material open items** that make two reasonable implementations differ visibly.\n- The EARS pattern is marked `unresolved` rather than guessing State-driven vs. Event-driven, because the mandatory/threshold decisions govern which pattern is correct.\n- Three grounded supporting requirements surface the boundary case (unknown age), the dismissal-path gap, and the observability gap — all kept outside the candidate list until accepted.","time":{"start":1791981835532,"end":1791981879040}}}
